{"run_id":"1795178121-590530344","line":18,"new":{"module_name":"help_test","snapshot_name":"shows_help","metadata":{"source":"tests/help_test.rs","assertion_line":18,"info":{"program":"bmm","args":["--help","--db-path","/tmp/.tmp3l1nWv/bmm.db"]}},"snapshot":"success: true\nexit_code: 0\n----- stdout -----\nbmm (stands for \"bookmarks manager\") lets you get to your bookmarks in a flash.\n\nIt does so by storing your bookmarks locally, allowing you to quickly access,\nmanage, and search through them using various commands.\n\nbmm has a traditional command line interface that can be used standalone or\nintegrated with other tools, and a textual user interface for easy browsing.\n\nUsage: bmm [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]\n\nCommands:\n  import    Import bookmarks from various sources\n  delete    Delete bookmarks\n  list      List bookmarks based on several kinds of queries\n  save      Save/update a bookmark\n  save-all  Save/update multiple bookmarks\n  search    Search bookmarks by matching over terms\n  show      Show bookmark details\n  tags      Interact with tags\n  check     Check bookmarks for broken/dead links\n  notes     Add, edit, or view a note attached to a bookmark\n  update    Check for (and install) a newer bmm binary at every location bmm is found on PATH\n  fetch     Fetch and print a website's description (the text a search engine would usually show underneath the link)\n  tui       Open bmm's TUI\n  help      Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)\n\nOptions:\n      --db-path <STRING>\n          Override bmm's database location (default: <DATA_DIR>/bmm/bmm.db)\n\n      --db <NAME>\n          Use one of your databases by name (as shown in the TUI's database list, eg. --db learning_linux), instead of the default bmm.db\n\n      --debug\n          Output debug information without doing anything\n\n  -h, --help\n          Print help (see a summary with '-h')\n\n  -V, --version\n          Print version\n\n----- stderr -----"},"old":{"module_name":"help_test","metadata":{},"snapshot":"success: true\nexit_code: 0\n----- stdout -----\nbmm (stands for \"bookmarks manager\") lets you get to your bookmarks in a flash.\n\nIt does so by storing your bookmarks locally, allowing you to quickly access,\nmanage, and search through them using various commands.\n\nbmm has a traditional command line interface that can be used standalone or\nintegrated with other tools, and a textual user interface for easy browsing.\n\nUsage: bmm [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]\n\nCommands:\n  import    Import bookmarks from various sources\n  delete    Delete bookmarks\n  list      List bookmarks based on several kinds of queries\n  save      Save/update a bookmark\n  save-all  Save/update multiple bookmarks\n  search    Search bookmarks by matching over terms\n  show      Show bookmark details\n  tags      Interact with tags\n  check     Check bookmarks for broken/dead links\n  notes     Add, edit, or view a note attached to a bookmark\n  tui       Open bmm's TUI\n  help      Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)\n\nOptions:\n      --db-path <STRING>\n          Override bmm's database location (default: <DATA_DIR>/bmm/bmm.db)\n\n      --debug\n          Output debug information without doing anything\n\n  -h, --help\n          Print help (see a summary with '-h')\n\n----- stderr -----"}}
